Description: Flexibly specify and fit Bayesian statistical models for epidemics. 'epidemia' leverages Rs formula interface so
    that users can parameterize reproduction numbers and ascertainment rates in terms of predictors. Infections are propagated over
    time using self-exciting point processes. Multiple regions can be modeled simultaneously with multilevel models. This is the
    continuation of the original 'epidemia' <https://github.com/ImperialCollegeLondon/epidemia>, now maintained by Swapnil Mishra;
    the model, its priors and its interface are the original authors' work and are described in
    the software paper Scott et al. (2021) <arXiv:2110.12461>. The underlying framework is developed in Bhatt et al. (2020) <arXiv:2012.00394> and
    applied in Mishra et al. (2022) <doi:10.1111/rssa.12988>. The design of the
    package has been inspired by, and has borrowed from, 'rstanarm' (Goodrich et al., 2020) <https://mc-stan.org/rstanarm/>. Models are fit
    using 'Stan' via the 'cmdstanr' back end, with posterior draws handled through the 'posterior' package. A Python port is included
    under 'python/'. Parts of the continuation, including the Python port, were developed with the assistance of Claude Code
    <https://claude.com/claude-code>, an AI coding tool from Anthropic.
